  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        498.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Marie Curie was born Maria Skłodowska on 7 November 1867 in Warsaw, then under Russian dominion, where women were denied university admission. Eager to pursue formal study, she emigrated to Paris in 1891, enrolling at the Sorbonne. On 26 July 1895 she wed the physicist Pierre Curie, forging a research partnership."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        391.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        490.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Henri Becquerel found that uranium emitted rays; she named this property of certain elements radioactivity. In July 1898 the Curies announced polonium, a tribute to her motherland, and on 26 December, radium. In a leaky shed they painstakingly processed tonnes of pitchblende, a uranium ore; by 1902 she had recovered about a tenth of a gram of radium chloride."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        283.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        382.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "On 10 December 1903 she jointly won the physics Nobel with Pierre and Becquerel, the first woman so recognised. The nomination had initially left her out; at Pierre's insistence, the omission was rectified. On 19 April 1906 a horse-drawn wagon fatally struck Pierre. On 5 November she gave her inaugural lecture as his successor, the Sorbonne's first woman professor."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        176.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        275.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "In November 1911 a press scandal about her private life erupted; she was urged to stay away. Undeterred, she collected the chemistry Nobel, won outright, on 10 December. Her distinction in two sciences remains unmatched. In the First World War she mobilised X-ray vans and stationary posts that examined over a million casualties."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        88.46141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        167.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "She died on 4 July 1934 of a blood disorder, probably caused by prolonged exposure to radiation. Her notebooks, still radioactive, are preserved in lead-lined boxes at the National Library of France; readers sign a liability waiver and don protective garments. The pages retain her handwriting and her radium."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "玛丽·居里原名玛丽亚·斯克沃多夫斯卡，1867年11月7日生于华沙。当时华沙处于俄国的统治之下，在那里，女性被剥夺了大学的入学资格。渴望追求正规的学习，她于1891年移居巴黎，注册入读索邦大学。1895年7月26日，她与物理学家皮埃尔·居里成婚，两人建立起研究上的合作关系。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "亨利·贝克勒尔发现，铀会发出射线；她把某些元素的这种性质/属性命名为放射性。1898年7月，居里夫妇宣布发现了钋，以此致敬她的祖国；12月26日，他们又宣布发现了镭。在一间漏雨的棚屋里，他们费尽心力地处理了成吨的沥青铀矿，这是一种铀矿石。到1902年，她已提取出约十分之一克的氯化镭。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        340.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1903年12月10日，她与皮埃尔和贝克勒尔共同获得诺贝尔物理学奖，第一位获此认可的女性。提名最初把她排除在外了；在皮埃尔的坚持下，这一疏漏得到了纠正。1906年4月19日，一辆马拉的货车致命地撞上了皮埃尔。11月5日，她作为他的继任者发表了就职演讲，成为索邦大学第一位女教授。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        252.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        332.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1911年11月，一场关于她私生活的媒体丑闻爆发，有人劝她不要前往。她并没有被吓住，于12月10日，领取了完全属于她的诺贝尔化学奖。她在两门科学中双双获得诺贝尔奖，这份荣誉至今仍无人企及。第一次世界大战期间，她动员/调用了X光面包车和固定站点，这些设施检查了超过一百万伤亡人员。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        163.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        243.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "1934年7月4日，她死于一种血液疾病，这很可能是由长期暴露于辐射所导致的。她的笔记本至今仍有放射性，保存在法国国家图书馆的铅衬的盒子里；读者须签署免责协议，并穿上防护服装。这些书页留存着她的笔迹，同样也留存着她所发现的镭。"
    }
  ],
